- GCD(A!, B!) = min(A, B)! because if A ≤ B, then A! divides B!, so GCD(A!, B!) = A!

The bug in the original code:
1. Uses `%I64d` which is Windows-specific (not standard for GNU C11 on Linux)
2. A and B can be up to 10^9, but min(A,B) ≤ 12, so we only need to compute factorial of min(A,B)
3. The factorial of 12 is 479001600 which fits in a long long, but computing factorial of large A/B would overflow

The fix is simple: compute factorial of min(A, B) and print it.

```c
#include<stdio.h>
int main()
{
    long long A, B, i;
    long long result = 1;
    scanf("%lld %lld", &A, &B);
    long long m = A < B ? A : B;
    for(i = 1; i <= m; i++)
    {
        result = result * i;
    }
    printf("%lld\n", result);
    return 0;
}
```

